			<div class="content" id="post-content-2883118">
									

<p>Love this. Hooray for loopholes!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;One&nbsp;nitpick</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">&nbsp;-&nbsp;But&nbsp;one&nbsp;that&nbsp;borders&nbsp;on&nbsp;creating&nbsp;a&nbsp;plot&nbsp;hole:</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Investigation has identified the remains of 51 human individuals in the wreckage of the vessel.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's actually really rare to find remains, even bones, in extremely old shipwrecks. There are a lot of scavengers on the ocean floor with a &quot;waste not, want not&quot; approach, bodies can become buoyant during decomposition and float away, people can get swept overboard and washed away as the ship sinks, or try to swim to shore like this guy and not make it&#8230; its really, really unlikely that they'd find all 51 other people.</p>
<p>It'd actually be better if you just left off that line and the</p>
<blockquote>
<p>whose remains have not been found in the wreckage</p>
</blockquote>
<p>thing from the next sentence. Raises the same questions without adding the &quot;hey wait a second&quot; trouble, and opens up some additional questions at the same time&#8230;</p>
</div>
</div>
